package mop;

import java.io.*;

/**
 * Companion to `unresolvable_owner`, guarding the other half of the resolution rule
 * (INV-ANA-40).
 *
 * The owner `StringBuilder` lives in `java.lang`, which no import of this spec registers.
 * It must resolve anyway, through the third and last resolution step — the package Java
 * leaves implicit — and the emitted target must RECORD that route, because the gator
 * attaches `MatchPolicy.STRICT` to exactly the targets that took it. The strictness is
 * what makes the step admissible: resolving such an owner leniently would match every
 * overload of the named method.
 *
 * `StringBuilder` rather than `String` on purpose: the real corpus case is
 * `RandomStringPassword.mop`, and a synthetic fixture that duplicated it would pass for
 * the wrong reason if the visitor ever special-cased that one class.
 *
 * This fixture lives under src/test/resources and is deliberately NOT part of any real
 * corpus.
 *
 * @severity error
 */

Implicit_StringBuilderOwner() {
	event bad_append before(Object o):
		call(* StringBuilder.append(..)) && target(o) {
   android.util.Log.v("RVSEC", __LOC + " ::: Implicit_StringBuilderOwner");
		}
}
